#461's headline is CONFIRMED to three digits: it claimed 337.1 ms and 1.37x at
8bpc t=1 against a base of 362.5; I measure 337.3 ms and 1.368x against a base
of 367.1 (my base is a fresh build of 6c17d8c; the 1.3% base disagreement moves
head/base from 0.9310 to 0.9187 and leaves the dav1d ratio unchanged).

BAND CHECK (the rule that killed a 2.7% claim last round). 7 of 8 cells are
DISJOINT base-vs-head. The exception is **v4k_8tile_10b t=8: base
[73.9..80.7] vs head [69.6..74.4] OVERLAP** — the medians differ 7.5% but the
ranges touch, so that single cell's delta does not clear its own noise at n=7.
It clears it in the separate n=5 decomposition sweep (75.5..77.4 vs 70.1..71.8,
disjoint), so the direction is not in doubt; the magnitude at that cell is.


2. THE ~1.30x BAR IS MET NOWHERE
---------------------------------
Best cell 1.368x (8bpc t=1), worst 1.948x (10bpc t=8). Against dav1d's SHIPPING
default (frame threading on) the multi-thread cells are worse still: 1.966x and
2.042x at t=8. What remains, in ms/frame, is the last column of the table
above: 16.7 ms at the best cell, 21-55 ms everywhere else.


3. SCALING DID NOT MOVE — as #460 says, and #461 admits
--------------------------------------------------------
t=1 -> t=8, same sweep:

  arm         v4k_8tile   v4k_8tile_10b
  base          4.928x        5.275x
  head          4.948x        5.212x
  dav1d_fd1     6.840x        6.694x
  dav1d_def     7.112x        7.019x

#460's target correction is INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCED: dav1d's tile-only model
(--framedelay 1) scales 6.84x here against 6.83x in its record, and its default
7.11x against 7.24x. Frame threading is worth 4% to dav1d at t=8 (34.7 vs 36.1).
6.84x, not 7.14x, is the comparable target.

CORRECTION TO THE CAMPAIGN'S OWN "5.15x". That figure is from the IN-PROCESS
instrument (scripts/perf/scaling_ab.sh). The two-point wall fit on the same
box, same day, idle, n=7 gives **4.93x** for base at 8bpc. Both are honest; they
are different instruments and the t=8 cell is the noisy end (base band
70.7..75.9). Quote the instrument with the number.


4. PER-BUCKET PROFILE at 8bpc t=1 (`sample`, 60 s, ~46.1k leaf samples/arm)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ms/frame = bucket fraction x that arm's measured ms/frame from section 1.

  bucket     base %   base ms/f    head %   head ms/f    delta
  entropy     59.74      219.3      64.99      219.2      -0.1
  tracker     18.00       66.1      12.35       41.7     -24.4
  kernels     18.54       68.1      18.68       63.0      -5.1
  runtime      1.06        3.9       1.15        3.9       0.0
  other        2.66        9.8       2.83        9.5      -0.3
  TOTAL                  367.1                 337.3     -29.8

Entropy is FLAT to 0.1 ms/frame — `decode_coefs` is 201.3 ms/frame before and
201.6 after — which is the control this whole table needs. The two `add`
instantiations alone go 30.4 + 24.7 = 55.1 ms/frame -> 17.5 + 14.0 = 31.5.

The kernels row is ATTRIBUTION SLOP, not a kernel win: no kernel changed.
Making `add`'s fast path a frameless leaf changes what LLVM inlines into
loopfilter/cdef call sites, so guard code that used to be a kernel leaf is now
a tracker leaf (or vice versa). The independent wall-clock decomposition below
settles it: the tracker's TOTAL cost falls 81.9 -> 52.2 ms/frame (-29.7), which
is the whole -29.8 ms wall delta. **All of the win is the tracker.**


