  addnop vs untracked at 8bpc t=1: 289.5 vs 286.4 — the CALL BARRIER is 3.1 ms
  of the tracker's 81.9, i.e. **3.8%** against #461's claimed 4.4%. Its central
  argument ("the call is 4%, `add`'s own latency is the rest") holds.

  Scaling with the tracker removed: untracked 6.015x vs base 5.019x at 8bpc —
  #460's 6.149x vs 5.077x, same instrument family, same conclusion.

  All four arms produced identical plane md5s at every cell.


6. SOUNDNESS — SIX mutations, each planted on the SHIPPING code, each restored
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Protocol: byte-level snapshot (sha256 58eb3296...) taken before any edit;
after every arm the file is restored from the snapshot and BOTH the sha256 and
`git diff --exit-code` are checked. Every restore came back exact.
All under `--features __probe_wide` (without it `wide_exclusion`'s anti-vacuity
assertion is compiled out).

  A  delete `add`'s IN-LOCK `state` re-read, FAST PATH ONLY (the 4af62ae TOCTOU)
     -> wide_exclusion FAILS on the real `witnesses` assertion, 8 missed overlaps.

  B  delete `add_contended`'s IN-LOCK `state` re-read, THAT SITE ALONE.
     -> wide_exclusion FAILS 5/5 runs, 1555 / 2960 / 2319 / 2906 / 2509 missed
        overlaps. #461 only ever deleted BOTH sites at once, which cannot tell
        whether the NEW cold path is gated. It is — and it is the DOMINANT one
        in that test (two orders of magnitude more witnesses than arm A).

  B' reachability control for B: `loop { spin_loop() }` at the top of
     `add_contended` HANGS the test (killed at 90 s, rc=124), so the path is
     genuinely entered rather than B being a lucky miss.
     Note also: an unconditional `panic!` in `add_contended` leaves
     wide_exclusion GREEN — correct, since a refusal cannot manufacture a
     missed overlap, but worth knowing before treating that gate as a
     liveness check for the contended path.

  C  drop `recs.allocated = occ | (1<<slot)` in `add`'s fast path, so the
     lock-protected superset stops growing before a publish (the new
     invariant `allocated ⊇ live`)
     -> 2 unit tests FAIL: `shard_overflow_promotes_and_still_detects`,
        `wide_and_narrow_still_see_each_other_on_a_one_shard_instance`.

  D  `alloc`'s new empty-shard fast path records the WRONG mutability
     (`self.mutable = 0` instead of `IS_MUT as u8` — the whole-byte store this
     branch introduced)
     -> shard_liveness FAILS 3 of 5.

  F  widen `live_mask`'s `allocated <= 1` straight-line shortcut to `<= 2`, so
     the mask becomes a SUBSET of the live set
     -> `narrow_release::a_lock_free_release_never_retires_a_live_neighbour`
        FAILS. ONLY that test — which is exactly the direction that file was
        written for.

  G  `mask == 0` fast path picks shard 1 instead of shard 0, so registration
     and release disagree
     -> 3 unit tests FAIL, incl. `serial_big_instance_keeps_strided_guards_narrow`.

`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` proved ACTIVELY, not by reading the attribute: an
`unsafe { core::ptr::null::<u8>().read() }` appended to `src/decode.rs` makes
the default build fail with `error: usage of an unsafe block`; restored to the
byte (sha bf667af1...), rebuild green.

Compile-time guard that survived the change: `size_of::<Shard>() == 128` still
asserts in release, so replacing one `AtomicU8` with `[AtomicU8; 7]` did NOT
grow the 128-shard table past 16 KiB. The build is the proof.

Attribute / assertion census, tracker_shard.rs, main -> composed: `#[inline*]`
40->47, `#[cold]` 8->11, `#[track_caller]` 11->13, `#[inline(never)]` 8->12,
`debug_assert` 9->10, `assert!` 23->23, `assert_eq!` 19->20, `#[test]` 19->19.
NOTHING decreased. Every removed line in the whole composed diff is accounted
for by the representation change (5 `occupied.load`, one `fetch_or`, one
`fetch_and`, two `&*`->`&mut *`, one restructured branch, one fmt reflow).

Threading: `mt_stress` 1/2/4/8/16 x 5 trials ok; `multi_decoder_pressure.sh`
12 procs x 3 iters x threads {1,2,4,8,16} x 5 vectors PASS, every md5 matching
the serial reference. `threaded_churn_leaks_no_slots` — the gate for the
per-slot-flag design's one new failure mode — 60/60 standalone runs.


